{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779394815,"nonce":"MJUOppjuqA+EP7UZibrPNqUyqEE=","message":"Additional authoring behavior to track here: port/schema constraints should eventually support optional auto-propagation, not only mismatch reporting.\n\nDesired UX: when a user wires/binds values whose schemas imply a vector shape, Studio should propagate that constraint into editable component params when safe. Examples:\n\n- Task Data signals are muxed/bound into `Network.input`; their combined vector size implies the network `input_size` / internal subgraph input dimension.\n- `Network.output` is wired to `PointMass.force` or `TwoLinkArm.force`; the mechanics port implies output size 2.\n- `Network.output` is wired to `Arm6MuscleRigidTendon.excitation`; the mechanics port implies output size 6.\n\nThis should not be hidden runtime mutation. It should be an authoring-side synchronization rule with a visible source of truth and likely a user setting, enabled by default for new graphs. If enabled, Studio updates the relevant parameter/subgraph dimensions automatically when the inferred constraint is unambiguous. If disabled, or if there are conflicting constraints, Studio should show the compatibility issue and let the user resolve it manually.\n\nExecution boundary: the worker/runtime should keep enforcing the final saved spec and fail clearly on mismatch. The auto-update behavior belongs in Studio authoring/validation, sharing the same PortSchema/ValueSchema constraint machinery.","files":null}]}